  <section class="pf-section" style="background:#f8fafc;">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>Bathroom Fitting in Liverpool's Victorian and Period Properties</h2>
      <p class="pf-sub">Liverpool has one of the highest concentrations of Victorian and Edwardian housing in England. We've fitted bathrooms across all of it.</p>

      <div class="pf-card pf-card-navy" style="margin-top:2rem;">
        <h3>The Challenges of Victorian Liverpool Bathrooms</h3>
        <p>Liverpool's Victorian terraces often have bathrooms in positions that were added as later extensions. Over an outrigger, converted from a small third bedroom, or installed in a ground-floor extension. Waste pipes in these positions usually require longer runs to connect to the soil stack. Existing joists may need reinforcement to support the weight of a freestanding bath or large vanity.</p>
        <p>Solid brick walls in Liverpool's Victorian housing cannot have pipes chased in as easily as cavity walls. Supply pipes are usually surface-run in neat boxing or routed under floorboards. Original cast iron waste pipes may require sections to be replaced where they've corroded. We identify all of this at the design consultation and include any necessary remedial work in the fixed-price quote before installation begins.</p>
        <p>For period properties where preserving original features matters, we plan the installation to avoid damaging original coving, picture rails, skirting boards and other period details wherever possible. We can also restore or replace features that have been damaged by previous renovations.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="pf-card pf-card-orange" style="margin-top:2rem;">
        <h3>What Works in a Liverpool Victorian Bathroom</h3>
        <p>Period and contemporary styles both work in Victorian properties when handled correctly. Traditional approach: a freestanding roll-top bath from BC Designs, high-cistern toilet, column basin, metro or hexagonal tiling, brushed brass or chrome brassware. Contemporary approach: clean modern fittings within a room that retains its period proportions and cornicing. Mixed metals work particularly well in period rooms where the textures and finishes give the room depth.</p>
        <p>We almost always advise underfloor heating in Victorian properties. The thermal mass of the solid brick walls means these rooms run cold without it. The cost premium is modest and the comfort transformation is significant. Pair it with a heated towel rail for a bathroom that feels warm from the moment you walk in.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

